ocs-authoring last) | Same. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Same. |\n\n**Deliberate non-edges:**\n\n- E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E004 (Rule 14): different components in different midstream repos (vllm, training-operator, kueue) with no shared build-against artifacts — all parallel.\n- E003 ∥ E004: same team but different components/repos; Kueue quota configuration and PyTorchJob ROCm support are complementary surfaces verified together end-to-end (AC on both), not serialized.\n- E001 ∥ E003/E004: the campaign gates serving productization (HLR-2) only. Training enablement (HLR-3, P1) delivers independent value and is not gated by serving parity results.\n- No `konflux-onboarding` epic: all components already exist in the platform and are Konflux-onboarded per architecture context; Rule 25 build-pipeline-green is an AC on E002, never an edge.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1 — validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing go/no-go scope | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-2 — supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | P0 | E002 |\n| HLR-3 — PyTorch training on ROCm via Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota | P1 | E003, E004 |\n| HLR-4 — documentation of supported hardware, drivers, validated model list | P2 | E005 |\n\nNo HLR is bundled: each maps to epics along clean component boundaries. HLR-3 spans two epics because Training Operator and Distributed Workloads are distinct components in distinct repos (Epic Boundary Rule 1) — job runtime support vs. quota/admission configuration.\n\nNote: E005 carries P1 despite HLR-4 being P2 — the `docs-authoring` priority exception derives priority from the strategy's frontmatter (`priority: Major` → P1).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were derived from architecture-context owning teams (RHOAI Serving for Serving Runtimes/KServe; Distributed Workloads for Training Operator/Distributed Workloads). Epic ACs were derived from HLR text.\n2. **Hardware access (open question 1) is an availability precondition, not an epic or AC**: procurement / partner-lab access for dedicated AMD Instinct nodes is granted by parties outside the strategy. It is recorded as a Risk on E001; E001's ACs sit on the deliverable (the validated report and reproducible environment configuration), which the epic's work does produce. If access is not secured, the campaign — and therefore all downstream work — slips; this is a schedule risk, not a DAG edge.\n3. **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: external dependency with uncertain timing, controlled by AMD and upstream communities. It does not change which epics exist — productization proceeds regardless and pins whatever ROCm image is released. Captured as a re-pinning AC on E002 plus a risk note; no DAG edge, no separate epic (no PR/RFC is submitted by us; Rule 21 precondition handling).\n4. **KServe is a passive component**: the strategy lists KServe as affected, but no KServe code change is implied — vLLM runtimes execute under existing KServe InferenceServices/ServingRuntime lifecycle. Validation that InferenceServices deploy and serve with the ROCm runtime is folded in as an AC on E002 (nearest active component, same owning team), per the passive-component rule.\n5. **Docs team assumption**: with no Impacted Teams table, E005 is assigned to RHOAI Serving (owner of the P0 serving scope and the validated model list). Confirm whether a documentation team should own it.\n6. **AMD node enablement folded into E001**: no platform component in the strategy's affected list owns an AMD GPU device plugin/operator (the platform's accelerator discovery today is the NVIDIA GPU Operator). Standing up the validation environment (ROCm driver stack, device plugin exposing amd.com/gpu) is part 