{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93d43363590798ec","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n10\t\n11\tAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training.\n12\t\n13\t## Epic List\n14\t\n15\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n16\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct ROCm validation campaign for vLLM serving parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support in the Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for ROCm workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n22\t\n23\t## Dependency DAG\n24\t\n25\t```mermaid\n26\tgraph TD\n27\t    E001[\"E001 ROCm validation campaign\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n28\t    E002 --> E005[\"E005 Documentation\"]\n29\t    E003[\"E003 PyTorchJob ROCm support\"] --> E005\n30\t    E004[\"E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E005\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\t## DAG Justification\n34\t\n35\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n36\t|------|------|-----------|\n37\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edge) | The campaign's go/no-go report determines which models the productized runtime must support — a true gate (`rewrite`: productization scope shrinks or descope if models fail parity). Recorded as `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n38\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n39\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Same. |\n40\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Same. |\n41\t\n42\t**Deliberate non-edges:**\n43\t\n44\t- E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E004 (Rule 14): different components in different midstream repos (vllm, training-operator, kueue) with no shared build-against artifacts — all parallel.\n45\t- E003 ∥ E004: same team but different components/repos; Kueue quota configuration and PyTorchJob ROCm support are complementary surfaces verified together end-to-end (AC on both), not serialized.\n46\t- E001 ∥ E003/E004: the campaign gates serving productization (HLR-2) only. Training enablement (HLR-3, P1) delivers independent value and is not gated by serving parity results.\n47\t- No `konflux-onboarding` epic: all components already exist in the platform and are Konflux-onboarded per architecture context; Rule 25 build-pipeline-green is an AC on E002, never an edge.\n48\t\n49\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n50\t\n51\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n52\t|-----|----------|---------|\n53\t| HLR-1 — validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing go/no-go scope | P0 | E001 |\n54\t| HLR-2 — supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | P0 | E002 |\n55\t| HLR-3 — PyTorch training on ROCm via Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota | P1 | E003, E004 |\n56\t| HLR-4 — documentation of supported hardware, drivers, validated model list | P2 | E005 |\n57\t\n58\tNo HLR is bundled: each maps to epics along clean component boundaries. HLR-3 spans two epics because Training Operator and Distributed Workloads are distinct components in distinct repos (Epic Boundary Rule 1) — job runtime support vs. quota/admission configuration.\n59\t\n60\tNote: E005 carries P1 despite HLR-4 being P2 — the `docs-authoring` priority exception derives priority from the strategy's frontmatter (`priority: Major` → P1).\n61\t\n62\t## Health Warnings\n63\t\n64\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were derived from architecture-context owning teams (RHOAI Serving for Serving Runtimes/KServe; Distributed Workloads for Training Operator/Distributed Workloads). Epic ACs were derived from HLR text.\n65\t2. **Hardware access (open question 1) is an availability precondition, not an epic or AC**: procurement / partner-lab access for dedicated AMD Instinct nodes is granted by parties outside the strategy. It is recorded as a Risk on E001; E001's ACs sit on the deliverable (the validated report and reproducible environment configuration), which the epic's work does produce. If access is not secured, the campaign — and therefore all downstream work — slips; this is a schedule risk, not a DAG edge.\n66\t3. **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: external dependency with uncertain timing, controlled by AMD and upstream communities. It does not change which epics exist — productization proceeds regardless and pins whatever ROCm image is released. Captured as a re-pinning AC on E002 plus a risk note; no DAG edge, no separate epic (no PR/RFC is submitted by us; Rule 21 precondition handling).\n67\t4. **KServe is a passive component**: the strategy lists KServe as affected, but no KServe code change is implied — vLLM runtimes execute under existing KServe InferenceServices/ServingRuntime lifecycle. Validation that InferenceServices deploy and serve with the ROCm runtime is folded in as an AC on E002 (nearest active component, same owning team), per the passive-component rule.\n68\t5. **Docs team assumption**: with no Impacted Teams table, E005 is assigned to RHOAI Serving (owner of the P0 serving scope and the validated model list). Confirm whether a documentation team should own it.\n69\t6. **AMD node enablement folded into E001**: no platform component in the strategy's affected list owns an AMD GPU device plugin/operator (the platform's accelerator discovery today is the NVIDIA GPU Operator). Standing up the validation environment (ROCm driver stack, device plugin exposing amd.com/gpu) is part of the campaign's scope. Conditional re-split trigger: if AMD GPU node enablement becomes a productized platform concern (an AMD GPU Operator equivalent under Platform Operator), split it out as a separate Implementation epic at that point.\n70\t\n71\t## Tiered Delivery\n72\t\n73\t- **Tier 1 (independent partial value)**: E001 — the validation campaign is risk-reduction work producing parity data and the go/no-go scope even if productization never follows. E003/E004 (training enablement, P1) are also independent of the serving-parity outcome and proceed in parallel.\n74\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 — serving-runtime productization, gated by the campaign's go/no-go report (rewrite gate: cleared-model scope). E005 (documentation) follows all implementations.\n75\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb8502ab256342e0","agent_id":"af63ff59559397d83","session_id":"e98bc21c-3e9c-4db6-bf4b-8cc0fb775cb8","uuid":"1521fac1-771d-4ba0-9e7f-f738ca53e6aa","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:41:59.738Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-93d43363590798ec","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
