{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b96cb4277198030f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n11\t\n12\tAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training.\n13\t\n14\t## Epic List\n15\t\n16\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n17\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct ROCm validation campaign for vLLM serving parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support in the Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for ROCm workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n23\t\n24\t## Dependency DAG\n25\t\n26\t```mermaid\n27\tgraph TD\n28\t    E001[\"E001 ROCm validation campaign\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n29\t    E002 --> E005[\"E005 Documentation\"]\n30\t    E003[\"E003 PyTorchJob ROCm support\"] --> E005\n31\t    E004[\"E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E005\n32\t```\n33\t\n34\t## DAG Justification\n35\t\n36\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n37\t|------|------|-----------|\n38\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edge) | The campaign's go/no-go report determines which models the productized runtime must support — a true gate (`rewrite`: productization scope shrinks or descope if models fail parity). Recorded as `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n39\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n40\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Same. |\n41\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Same. |\n42\t\n43\t**Deliberate non-edges:**\n44\t\n45\t- E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E004 (Rule 14): different components in different midstream repos (vllm, training-operator, kueue) with no shared build-against artifacts — all parallel.\n46\t- E003 ∥ E004: same team but different components/repos; Kueue quota configuration and PyTorchJob ROCm support are complementary surfaces verified together end-to-end (AC on both), not serialized.\n47\t- E001 ∥ E003/E004: the campaign gates serving productization (HLR-2) only. Training enablement (HLR-3, P1) delivers independent value and is not gated by serving parity results. Environment coordination (not a DAG edge): E003/E004 verification ACs run on AMD Instinct nodes whose stand-up (ROCm driver stack, `amd.com/gpu` device plugin) is in E001's scope — training verification either shares that environment once stood up or procures its own AMD nodes; recorded as Risks on both training epics.\n48\t- No `konflux-onboarding` epic: all components already exist in the platform and are Konflux-onboarded per architecture context; Rule 25 build-pipeline-green is an AC on E002, never an edge.\n49\t\n50\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n51\t\n52\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n53\t|-----|----------|---------|\n54\t| HLR-1 — validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing go/no-go scope | P0 | E001 |\n55\t| HLR-2 — supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | P0 | E002 |\n56\t| HLR-3 — PyTorch training on ROCm via Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota | P1 | E003, E004 |\n57\t| HLR-4 — documentation of supported hardware, drivers, validated model list | P2 | E005 |\n58\t\n59\tNo HLR is bundled: each maps to epics along clean component boundaries. HLR-3 spans two epics because Training Operator and Distributed Workloads are distinct components in distinct repos (Epic Boundary Rule 1) — job runtime support vs. quota/admission configuration.\n60\t\n61\tNote: E005 carries P1 despite HLR-4 being P2 — the `docs-authoring` priority exception derives priority from the strategy's frontmatter (`priority: Major` → P1).\n62\t\n63\t## Health Warnings\n64\t\n65\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were derived from architecture-context owning teams (RHOAI Serving for Serving Runtimes/KServe; Distributed Workloads for Training Operator/Distributed Workloads). Epic ACs were derived from HLR text.\n66\t2. **Hardware access (open question 1) is an availability precondition, not an epic or AC**: procurement / partner-lab access for dedicated AMD Instinct nodes is granted by parties outside the strategy. It is recorded as a Risk on E001; E001's ACs sit on the deliverable (the validated report and reproducible environment configuration), which the epic's work does produce. If access is not secured, the campaign — and therefore all downstream work — slips; this is a schedule risk, not a DAG edge.\n67\t3. **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: external dependency with uncertain timing, controlled by AMD and upstream communities. It does not change which epics exist — productization proceeds regardless and pins whatever ROCm image is released. Captured as a re-pinning AC on E002 plus a risk note; no DAG edge, no separate epic (no PR/RFC is submitted by us; Rule 21 precondition handling).\n68\t4. **KServe is a passive component**: the strategy lists KServe as affected, but no KServe code change is implied — vLLM runtimes execute under existing KServe InferenceServices/ServingRuntime lifecycle. Validation that InferenceServices deploy and serve with the ROCm runtime is folded in as an AC on E002 (nearest active component, same owning team), per the passive-component rule.\n69\t5. **Docs team assumption**: with no Impacted Teams table, E005 is assigned to RHOAI Serving (owner of the P0 serving scope and the validated model list). Confirm whether a documentation team should own it.\n70\t6. **AMD node enablement folded into E001**: no platform component in the strategy's affected list owns an AMD GPU device plugin/operator (the platform's accelerator discovery today is the NVIDIA GPU Operator). Standing up the validation environment (ROCm driver stack, device plugin exposing amd.com/gpu) is part of the campaign's scope. Conditional re-split trigger: if AMD GPU node enablement becomes a productized platform concern (an AMD GPU Operator equivalent under Platform Operator), split it out as a separate Implementation epic at that point.\n71\t\n72\t## Tiered Delivery\n73\t\n74\t- **Tier 1 (independent partial value)**: E001 — the validation campaign is risk-reduction work producing parity data and the go/no-go scope even if productization never follows. E003/E004 (training enablement, P1) are also independent of the serving-parity outcome and proceed in parallel.\n75\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 — serving-runtime productization, gated by the campaign's go/no-go report (rewrite gate: cleared-model scope). E005 (documentation) follows all implementations.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b74620e441716e6d","agent_id":"aca7094c6bd436c1b","session_id":"e98bc21c-3e9c-4db6-bf4b-8cc0fb775cb8","uuid":"df7b421d-6045-438e-bb02-1d1bc539a1c3","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:42:57.327Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b96cb4277198030f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9020"}
